    },
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p27-task67-72-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=\\sqrt{\\frac{x+1}{x-1}}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$D=(-\\infty,-1]\\cup(1,\\infty)$; $R=[0,1)\\cup(1,\\infty)$.\n\nUse $\\frac{x+1}{x-1}=1+\\frac{2}{x-1}$ to find the output intervals on the two domain branches.",
          "worked": "The radicand must be non-negative and its denominator non-zero:\n$$\\frac{x+1}{x-1}\\ge0,\\qquad x\\ne1.$$\nFor $x>1$, the numerator and denominator are both positive. For $x<1$, the denominator is negative, so the numerator must be non-positive: $x+1\\le0$. Hence\n$$D=(-\\infty,-1]\\cup(1,\\infty).$$\nRewrite the radicand:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{x+1}{x-1}&=\\frac{x-1+2}{x-1}\\\\\\frac{x+1}{x-1}&=1+\\frac{2}{x-1}.\\end{align*}$$\nFor $x\\le-1$, the term $\\frac{2}{x-1}$ takes every value in $[-1,0)$, so the radicand takes every value in $[0,1)$. Taking its square root gives $[0,1)$.\n\nFor $x>1$, the term $\\frac{2}{x-1}$ takes every positive value, so the radicand and its square root each take every value in $(1,\\infty)$.\n\nTherefore\n$$R=[0,1)\\cup(1,\\infty).$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 62
      },
      {
        "id": "p27-task67-72-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=\\frac{\\sqrt{x-1}}{\\sqrt{4-x}}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$D=[1,4)$; $R=[0,\\infty)$.\n\nOn this domain, $f(x)^2=\\frac{3}{4-x}-1$.",
          "worked": "The numerator permits a zero radicand, but the radical denominator must be positive:\n$$\\begin{align*}x-1&\\ge0\\\\x&\\ge1\\\\4-x&>0\\\\x&<4.\\end{align*}$$\nTheir intersection gives\n$$D=[1,4).$$\nOn this domain, $0<4-x\\le3$ and $f(x)\\ge0$. Also,\n$$\\begin{align*}f(x)^2&=\\frac{x-1}{4-x}\\\\f(x)^2&=\\frac{3}{4-x}-1.\\end{align*}$$\nAs $4-x$ takes every value in $(0,3]$, the expression $\\frac{3}{4-x}-1$ takes every value in $[0,\\infty)$. Its non-negative square root therefore has range\n$$R=[0,\\infty).$$\nThe value zero occurs at $x=1$, and the outputs grow without bound as $x$ approaches $4$ from below.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 62
      },
      {
        "id": "p27-task67-72-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=\\frac{\\sqrt{x^2-1}}{\\sqrt{x+1}}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$D=[1,\\infty)$; $R=[0,\\infty)$.\n\nEstablish the original domain before simplifying to $\\sqrt{x-1}$.",
          "worked": "The numerator requires\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2-1&\\ge0\\\\(x-1)(x+1)&\\ge0.\\end{align*}$$\nThus $x\\le-1$ or $x\\ge1$. The denominator also requires\n$$\\begin{align*}x+1&>0\\\\x&>-1.\\end{align*}$$\nThe intersection is $x\\ge1$, so\n$$D=[1,\\infty).$$\nOn this original domain,\n$$\\begin{align*}f(x)&=\\sqrt{\\frac{(x-1)(x+1)}{x+1}}\\\\f(x)&=\\sqrt{x-1}.\\end{align*}$$\nHere $x-1$ takes every non-negative value, so\n$$R=[0,\\infty).$$\nThe cancellation does not admit $x=-1$, where the original denominator is zero.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 68
      },
      {
        "id": "p27-task67-72-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=x+\\sqrt{x}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$D=[0,\\infty)$; $R=[0,\\infty)$.\n\nBoth terms increase from zero.",
          "worked": "The square root requires $x\\ge0$, so\n$$D=[0,\\infty).$$\nOn this domain, both terms are non-negative:\n$$\\begin{align*}f(x)&=x+\\sqrt{x}\\ge0\\\\f(0)&=0.\\end{align*}$$\nThe sum is continuous and increases from zero without an upper bound. Every non-negative output therefore occurs, giving\n$$R=[0,\\infty).$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 44
      },
      {
        "id": "p27-task67-72-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{8-x}}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$D=(0,8)$; $R=[1,\\infty)$.\n\nSet $p=\\sqrt{x(8-x)}$, so $0<p\\le4$. Then $f(x)^2-1=\\frac{(4-p)(p+2)}{p^2}\\ge0$, with equality at $x=4$.",
          "worked": "Both radical denominators must be positive:\n$$x>0,\\qquad 8-x>0.$$\nHence\n$$D=(0,8).$$\nPut $p=\\sqrt{x(8-x)}>0$. Completing the square gives\n$$\\begin{align*}p^2&=x(8-x)\\\\p^2&=16-(x-4)^2\\\\p^2&\\le16.\\end{align*}$$\nThus $0<p\\le4$. Squaring the positive function gives\n$$\\begin{align*}f(x)^2&=\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{8-x}+\\frac{2}{p}\\\\f(x)^2&=\\frac{8}{p^2}+\\frac{2}{p}\\\\f(x)^2&=\\frac{8+2p}{p^2}.\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore\n$$\\begin{align*}f(x)^2-1&=\\frac{8+2p-p^2}{p^2}\\\\f(x)^2-1&=\\frac{(4-p)(p+2)}{p^2}\\\\f(x)^2-1&\\ge0.\\end{align*}$$\nSince $f(x)>0$, this gives $f(x)\\ge1$. Equality occurs when $p=4$, namely at $x=4$, where\n$$f(4)=\\frac12+\\frac12=1.$$\nThe function is continuous on $(0,8)$ and grows without bound near either excluded endpoint. Consequently every value from 1 upwards occurs:\n$$R=[1,\\infty).$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 102
      },
      {
        "id": "p27-task67-72-f",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "f",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{4-x}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$D=[0,4]$; $R=[2,2\\sqrt2]$.\n\nSquare the non-negative sum and use $x(4-x)=4-(x-2)^2$.",
          "worked": "Both radicands must be non-negative:\n$$x\\ge0,\\qquad 4-x\\ge0.$$\nHence\n$$D=[0,4].$$\nLet $y=f(x)$, so $y\\ge0$. Then\n$$\\begin{align*}y^2&=(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{4-x})^2\\\\y^2&=4+2\\sqrt{x(4-x)}.\\end{align*}$$\nOn the domain,\n$$x(4-x)=4-(x-2)^2,$$\nso\n$$\\begin{align*}0&\\le x(4-x)\\le4\\\\0&\\le\\sqrt{x(4-x)}\\le2\\\\4&\\le y^2\\le8\\\\2&\\le y\\le2\\sqrt2.\\end{align*}$$\nThe lower bound occurs at $x=0$ or $x=4$, and the upper bound occurs at $x=2$:\n$$\\begin{align*}f(0)&=f(4)=2\\\\f(2)&=2\\sqrt2.\\end{align*}$$\nContinuity supplies every value between these bounds. Therefore\n$$R=[2,2\\sqrt2].$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 84
      }
    ]
